9×9. In pair 3, 5×5 becomes 25×25. So the output is an n×n arrangement of n×n blocks, with one block for each input cell. The question is what goes into the block for input cell (i, j).\n\n## What each block contains\n\n**Pair 1.** The input is `666 / 688 / 886`.\n- Cell (0,0) is colour 6. The top-left output block is `666 / 600 / 006`. That is the input with every non-6 cell turned into 0.\n- Cell (1,1) is colour 8. Its block (rows 3–5, cols 3–5) is `000 / 088 / 880`. That is the input with every non-8 cell turned into 0.\n\n**Pair 2.** The input is `244 / 324 / 332`.\n- Cell (0,0) is colour 2. Its block is `200 / 020 / 002`, so only the 2-diagonal is kept.\n- Cell (0,1) is colour 4. Its block is `044 / 004 / 000`, so only the 4s are kept.\n- Cell (1,0) is colour 3. Its block keeps only the 3s.\n\n**Pair 3.** The 5×5 input works the same way.\n- Every colour-1 cell gets the input with its 8s replaced by 0.\n- Every colour-8 cell gets the input with its 1s replaced by 0. For example, block (1,1) starts `00000 / 08880 …`.\n\n**Rule:** block (i, j) is the input grid with only the cells equal to `input[i][j]` kept. Every other cell becomes 0.\n\nThe rule does not depend on the background or on any particular colour. Each cell simply decides \"show me where my colour appears in the whole grid\". The fill colour 0 is the same in every pair.\n\n## Applying it to the tests\n\n**Test 1** (5×5, colours 1, 2, 4) gives a 25×25 output.\n- Each colour-1 cell gets a block showing only the 1s.\n- Each colour-4 cell gets a block showing only the 4s, including the isolated 4 at the bottom-left.\n- Each colour-2 cell gets a block showing only the 2s.\n\n**Test 2** (4×4, colours 3, 4, 6) gives a 16×16 output with the same three kinds of blocks.\n\n## Construction stages\n\n1. Allocate an all-0 canvas of size n²×n².\n2. Stamp the blocks one block-row at a time, emitting after each row.\n3. Emit the finished grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    n, m = g.shape\n    out = np.zeros((n * n, m * m), dtype=int)\n    emit(out, f\"Allocate a {n*n}x{m*m} canvas of 0s: one {n}x{m} block per input cell.\")\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(m):\n            c = g[i, j]\n            block = np.where(g == c, g, 0)\n            out[i*n:(i+1)*n, j*m:(j+1)*m] = block\n        emit(out, f\"Block-row {i}: each cell's block is the input showing only that cell's colour \"\n                  f\"(colours {list(map(int, g[i]))}), all other cells 0.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every block shows where its cell's colour occurs in the input.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Stamp the blocks one block-row at a time, emitting after each row."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each input is a small n×n grid, and its output is exactly (n·n)×(n·n)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Allocate an all-0 canvas of size n²×n²."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Allocate a 9x9 canvas of 0s: one 3x3 block per input cell."}],"scale":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block-row 0: each cell's block is the input showing only that cell's colour (colours [6, 6, 6]), all other cells 0."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block-row 1: each cell's block is the input showing only that cell's colour (colours [6, 8, 8]), all other cells 0."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block-row 2: each cell's block is the input showing only that cell's colour (colours [8, 8, 6]), all other cells 0."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"e1d95d998f5a39dd57964b8d7252a435af2d909c6fb765b7da5708d1aa1473d8","task_sha256":"8b79e258939eb644600311c6ca5266458dcdc973774fea367e6cc44ecb68c53f","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
